{
  "scenes": [
    {
      "source": "S1",
      "mode": "explain",
      "narration": "Kinematics involves describing motion through properties such as position, time, velocity, and acceleration.",
      "focus": "describing motion through properties such as position",
      "keep": "Kinematics is the description of motion without considering its causes."
    },
    {
      "source": "S2",
      "mode": "explain",
      "narration": "The change in position of an object is called displacement. Since displacement indicates direction, it is a vector and can be positive or negative.",
      "focus": "change in position is called displacement",
      "keep": "Displacement gives the change in position along a straight line."
    },
    {
      "source": "S4",
      "mode": "explain",
      "narration": "Displacement is written as delta x equals final position minus initial position. The SI units of displacement are meters.",
      "focus": "",
      "keep": "Displacement is a vector quantity that can be positive or negative."
    },
    {
      "source": "S7",
      "mode": "listen",
      "narration": "",
      "focus": "And the key point here is that average velocity depends on whatever time interval you're referring to.",
      "keep": ""
    }
  ],
  "example": [
    {
      "source": "E1",
      "narration": "We are given position and time in the wording of the problem so we can calculate the displacements and the elapsed time.",
      "focus": "calculate the displacements and the elapsed time",
      "keep": ""
    },
    {
      "source": "E2",
      "narration": "The average velocity is equal to the total displacement divided by the elapsed time, which gives minus zero point zero thirteen kilometers per minute.",
      "focus": "",
      "keep": ""
    },
    {
      "source": "E3",
      "narration": "Jills total displacement means at the end of her trip she ends up zero point seven five kilometers due west of her home.",
      "focus": "Jills total displacement is",
      "keep": ""
    }
  ],
  "mistake": "Students often confuse displacement with total distance or total path length by adding the initial and final positions together. The correct method is subtracting the initial position from the final position."
}

{
  "scenes": [
    {
      "source": "S1",
      "mode": "explain",
      "narration": "The instantaneous velocity of an object is the limit of the average velocity as the elapsed time approaches zero.",
      "focus": "INSTANTANEOUS VELOCITY The instantaneous velocity of an object is",
      "keep": "Instantaneous velocity is the limit of average velocity as elapsed time approaches zero."
    },
    {
      "source": "S4",
      "mode": "explain",
      "narration": "We can calculate instantaneous velocity by taking the derivative of the position function with respect to time.",
      "focus": "",
      "keep": ""
    },
    {
      "source": "S6",
      "mode": "explain",
      "narration": "On a graph of position versus time, the instantaneous velocity is the slope of the tangent line at a given point.",
      "focus": "",
      "keep": "The slope of a position time graph at a specific time gives instantaneous velocity."
    },
    {
      "source": "S7",
      "mode": "listen",
      "narration": "",
      "focus": "And that's how we describe instantaneous velocity at some specific time. If we were now being a little bit more general, we could just say that v at any time t is the limit delta t goes to 0, delta x over delta t, all direction i hat. And the only thing here is we're no longer considering t1, but an arbitrary time t. This quantity, the limit, is awkward to write every time. It has a name, and that's precisely what we call the derivative of the position function. So our instantaneous velocity is the time derivative of the position function at any instant in time.",
      "keep": ""
    }
  ],
  "example": [
    {
      "source": "E1",
      "narration": "We are given a position versus time graph and need to find the velocity during each time interval by taking the slope.",
      "focus": "Strategy The graph contains three straight lines during three time intervals",
      "keep": ""
    },
    {
      "source": "E2",
      "narration": "For the first interval, the average velocity is zero point five meters minus zero meters divided by zero point five seconds minus zero seconds, giving one meter per second.",
      "focus": "Time interval 0 s to 0.5 s",
      "keep": ""
    },
    {
      "source": "E3",
      "narration": "During the first interval, the position is moving away from the origin and the slope is positive one meter per second.",
      "focus": "During the time interval between 0 s and 0.5 s",
      "keep": "The instantaneous velocity can be found by taking the slope of the curve."
    }
  ],
  "mistake": "Do not confuse instantaneous velocity with the area under the curve. The correct representation on a position versus time graph is the slope of the tangent line at that point."
}

{
  "scenes": [
    {
      "source": "S3",
      "mode": "explain",
      "narration": "Acceleration is the rate at which velocity changes. It has both magnitude and direction.",
      "focus": "Acceleration is the rate at which velocity changes",
      "keep": "Acceleration is the rate at which velocity changes."
    },
    {
      "source": "S1",
      "mode": "explain",
      "narration": "Instantaneous acceleration is the acceleration at a specific instant in time. We calculate the average acceleration between two points and let the time interval approach zero.",
      "focus": "acceleration at a specific instant in time",
      "keep": ""
    },
    {
      "source": "S5",
      "mode": "explain",
      "narration": "In a graph of velocity versus time, instantaneous acceleration is the slope of the tangent line.",
      "focus": "",
      "keep": "Instantaneous acceleration is the slope of the tangent line on a velocity-versus-time graph."
    },
    {
      "source": "S7",
      "mode": "listen",
      "narration": "",
      "focus": "And this quantity here, which is delta v over delta t, again, we can even call, as we said before, we can call this average acceleration. But what we're interested in is as we take the limit as delta t goes to 0, then the slope changes, you can see.",
      "keep": ""
    }
  ],
  "example": [
    {
      "source": "E1",
      "narration": "We need to find the functional form of acceleration, calculate instantaneous velocity and acceleration at specific times, and interpret their directions.",
      "focus": "",
      "keep": ""
    },
    {
      "source": "E2",
      "narration": "We find the acceleration function by taking the derivative of the velocity function. Then we calculate values from these functions and compare directions.",
      "focus": "",
      "keep": ""
    },
    {
      "source": "E3",
      "narration": "Taking the derivative gives the acceleration function as twenty minus ten times t. At two seconds, velocity is at a maximum and acceleration is zero.",
      "focus": "",
      "keep": "Instantaneous acceleration is calculated from the derivative of the velocity function."
    }
  ],
  "mistake": "Students often confuse the slope of the tangent line at an instant with the slope of a secant line over a time interval. The slope of the tangent line gives the instantaneous acceleration."
}

{
  "scenes": [
    {
      "source": "S4",
      "mode": "explain",
      "narration": "The kinematic equations can be used with any particle under uniform acceleration.",
      "focus": "",
      "keep": "The kinematic equations can be used with any particle under uniform acceleration."
    },
    {
      "source": "S1",
      "mode": "explain",
      "narration": "Before we get into the examples, let us look at some of the equations more closely to see the behavior of acceleration at extreme values.",
      "focus": "Before we get into the examples",
      "keep": "We need as many equations as there are unknowns to solve a given situation."
    },
    {
      "source": "S2",
      "mode": "explain",
      "narration": "The time and distance required for car one to catch car two depends on the initial distance as well as the velocities and acceleration.",
      "focus": "The time and distance required for",
      "keep": ""
    },
    {
      "source": "S6",
      "mode": "listen",
      "narration": "",
      "focus": "And then when it breaks, well, we know it decelerates. So we're definitely going to have a minus sign.",
      "keep": ""
    }
  ],
  "example": [
    {
      "source": "E1",
      "narration": "We want to find how far a dragster travels in five point five six seconds when starting from rest at twenty six point zero meters per second squared.",
      "focus": "",
      "keep": ""
    },
    {
      "source": "E2",
      "narration": "First, let us draw a sketch. We use the displacement equation when we identify initial velocity, acceleration, and time.",
      "focus": "First, let us draw a sketch",
      "keep": ""
    },
    {
      "source": "E3",
      "narration": "Starting from rest means initial velocity is zero, and substituting the known values gives a final distance of four hundred two meters.",
      "focus": "",
      "keep": ""
    },
    {
      "source": "E4",
      "narration": "Converting four hundred two meters to miles shows it is close to one quarter of a mile, making our answer reasonable.",
      "focus": "",
      "keep": ""
    }
  ],
  "mistake": "Students often confuse the linear time dependence of velocity with the quadratic time dependence of displacement. The correct equation relating velocity, constant acceleration, and time is velocity equals initial velocity plus acceleration times time."
}

{
  "scenes": [
    {
      "source": "S1",
      "mode": "explain",
      "narration": "An object falling without air resistance or friction is defined to be in free fall.",
      "focus": "an object falling without air resistance or friction is defined to be in free fall",
      "keep": "An object falling without air resistance or friction is in free fall."
    },
    {
      "source": "S2",
      "mode": "explain",
      "narration": "If an object is dropped, the initial velocity is zero when in free fall, and the motion has constant acceleration.",
      "focus": "the initial velocity is zero when in free fall",
      "keep": ""
    },
    {
      "source": "S3",
      "mode": "explain",
      "narration": "On Earth, all free falling objects have an acceleration due to gravity that averages nine point eight one meters per second squared.",
      "focus": "On Earth, all free-falling objects have an acceleration g due to gravity",
      "keep": "All free falling objects on Earth have a constant acceleration due to gravity."
    },
    {
      "source": "S7",
      "mode": "listen",
      "narration": "",
      "focus": "When the object was at its highest point, was its velocity zero? Was it its acceleration zero or non-zero?",
      "keep": ""
    }
  ],
  "example": [
    {
      "source": "E1",
      "narration": "We find how much time elapses before the ball reaches the ground, and its velocity when it arrives.",
      "focus": "How much time elapses before the ball reaches the ground",
      "keep": ""
    },
    {
      "source": "E2",
      "narration": "We choose the origin at the top of the building with the positive direction upward and negative downward.",
      "focus": "Choose the origin at the top of the building with the positive direction upward and the negative direction downward",
      "keep": ""
    },
    {
      "source": "E3",
      "narration": "Substituting the given values gives a quadratic equation with roots of negative five seconds and four seconds.",
      "focus": "",
      "keep": ""
    },
    {
      "source": "E4",
      "narration": "The positive root is correct because time zero corresponds to when the ball was released.",
      "focus": "the negative root would correspond to a time before the ball was released, which is not physically meaningful",
      "keep": "The physical significance of quadratic equation roots helps determine the correct time."
    }
  ],
  "mistake": "Students often confuse the unrounded value with the two significant figure average. The correct average magnitude of acceleration due to gravity is nine point eight meters per second squared when rounded to two significant figures."
}

{
  "scenes": [
    {
      "source": "S1",
      "mode": "explain",
      "narration": "Integral calculus gives us a more complete formulation of kinematics when acceleration is a known function of time.",
      "focus": "Let us begin with a particle with an acceleration",
      "keep": "Integral calculus gives us a more complete formulation of kinematics."
    },
    {
      "source": "S2",
      "mode": "explain",
      "narration": "We can take the indefinite integral of both sides to find velocity and position equations, including constants of integration.",
      "focus": "where C one is a constant of integration",
      "keep": "If acceleration is known we can use integral calculus to derive expressions for velocity and position."
    },
    {
      "source": "S6",
      "mode": "explain",
      "narration": "Graphs of velocity and position show how the motorboat decreases its velocity to zero and eventually reverses direction.",
      "focus": "",
      "keep": "If acceleration is constant the integral equations reduce to motion with constant acceleration."
    },
    {
      "source": "S7",
      "mode": "listen",
      "narration": "",
      "focus": "That is handy and from that we will find that v of t equals simply v zero All good Now we need to integrate this to get to our position function",
      "keep": ""
    }
  ],
  "example": [
    {
      "source": "E1",
      "narration": "A motorboat travels at a constant velocity of five point zero meters per second when it starts to accelerate opposite to the motion. We need to find the velocity function, time to zero velocity, position function, displacement, and graph them.",
      "focus": "Motion of a Motorboat A motorboat is traveling at a constant velocity",
      "keep": ""
    },
    {
      "source": "E2",
      "narration": "To get the velocity and position functions we must integrate and use initial conditions to find the constants of integration. We set velocity to zero to find the time, and evaluate the position function at that time for displacement.",
      "focus": "Strategy To get the velocity function we must integrate",
      "keep": ""
    },
    {
      "source": "E3",
      "narration": "By integrating the acceleration function and applying initial conditions, we find the velocity function is five point zero minus one eighth t squared, reaching zero at six point three seconds, and the position function is five point zero t minus one twenty fourth t cubed.",
      "focus": "",
      "keep": ""
    }
  ],
  "mistake": "Students often confuse going backward from acceleration with going forward via derivatives. While taking the derivative of velocity gives acceleration, we use integral calculus to work backward from acceleration to find the velocity function."
}

{
  "scenes": [
    {
      "source": "S1",
      "mode": "explain",
      "narration": "A free body diagram is a sketch showing all external forces acting on an object or system.",
      "focus": "a sketch showing all external forces acting on an object or system",
      "keep": "A free body diagram is a sketch showing all external forces acting on an object."
    },
    {
      "source": "S2",
      "mode": "explain",
      "narration": "The system is represented by a single isolated point, and the forces are represented by vectors extending outward from that point.",
      "focus": "represented by a single isolated point, and the forces are represented by vectors",
      "keep": ""
    },
    {
      "source": "S4",
      "mode": "explain",
      "narration": "All external forces acting on the car are shown. The forces acting on the car are transferred to a coordinate plane for simpler analysis.",
      "focus": "",
      "keep": ""
    },
    {
      "source": "S7",
      "mode": "listen",
      "narration": "",
      "focus": "And now we have to analyze the forces on the block as it's sliding down. There is a friction with a coefficient mu k. And so we have the gravitational force, mg.",
      "keep": ""
    }
  ],
  "example": [
    {
      "source": "E1",
      "narration": "Construct the free body diagram for object A and object B. Assume that the rope pulls object A so that it slides upward, and that object B is sliding down the slope.",
