  "mistake": "Students often include the environment instead of isolating the object. To draw a free body diagram, you start by isolating the object of interest, often representing it as a dot or a simple shape."
}

{
  "scenes": [
    {
      "source": "S3",
      "mode": "explain",
      "narration": "Inertia is the tendency of an object to remain at rest or remain in motion.",
      "focus": "Inertia is the tendency of an object to remain at rest or remain in motion.",
      "keep": "Inertia is the tendency of an object to remain at rest or remain in motion."
    },
    {
      "source": "S1",
      "mode": "explain",
      "narration": "Newton's first law says that the velocity of an object remains constant if the net force on it is zero.",
      "focus": "the velocity of an object remains constant if the net force on it is zero",
      "keep": "The velocity of an object remains constant if the net force on it is zero."
    },
    {
      "source": "S4",
      "mode": "explain",
      "narration": "A car parked has a velocity of zero and a net force of zero.",
      "focus": "",
      "keep": ""
    },
    {
      "source": "S6",
      "mode": "listen",
      "narration": "",
      "focus": "Such a coordinate system is called an inertial coordinate system. So another way of stating Newton's first law is that inertial coordinate systems exist.",
      "keep": "An inertial coordinate system is one in which an isolated body moves at constant velocity."
    }
  ],
  "example": [
    {
      "source": "E1",
      "narration": "Your car is parked outside your house, and then moves at constant velocity down the street. Does Newton's first law apply in these situations?",
      "focus": "Does Newton's first law apply in this situation",
      "keep": ""
    },
    {
      "source": "E2",
      "narration": "For the parked car, we consider a body at rest. For the moving car, we look at a body in motion.",
      "focus": "dealing with a body at rest",
      "keep": ""
    },
    {
      "source": "E3",
      "narration": "When your car is parked, all forces are balanced and acceleration is zero. When moving at constant velocity, the net force is also zero because friction opposes drag.",
      "focus": "all forces on the car must be balanced",
      "keep": "When forces on a system are balanced, the net force is zero."
    }
  ],
  "mistake": "Students often confuse inertial frames with Earth-fixed coordinates. The correct answer is that an inertial coordinate system is one in which an isolated body maintains a constant velocity and does not experience acceleration relative to that observer."
}

{
  "scenes": [
    {
      "source": "S1",
      "mode": "explain",
      "narration": "The acceleration of an object depends only on the net external force and the mass of the object.",
      "focus": "the acceleration of an object depends only on the net external force",
      "keep": "The acceleration of an object depends on the net external force and its mass."
    },
    {
      "source": "S2",
      "mode": "explain",
      "narration": "The acceleration of a system is directly proportional to the net external force and inversely proportional to its mass.",
      "focus": "The acceleration of a system is directly proportional to",
      "keep": ""
    },
    {
      "source": "S6",
      "mode": "explain",
      "narration": "A car experiences two force vectors originating from its center, pointing in different directions.",
      "focus": "",
      "keep": ""
    },
    {
      "source": "S7",
      "mode": "listen",
      "narration": "",
      "focus": "If a force acts on a body, then it will accelerate in proportion to that force.",
      "keep": "A force acting on a body causes it to accelerate in proportion to that force."
    }
  ],
  "example": [
    {
      "source": "E1",
      "narration": "We want to find the acceleration of a lawn mower when a net external force of fifty-one newtons acts on its mass of twenty-four kilograms.",
      "focus": "What Acceleration Can a Person Produce When Pushing a Lawn Mower",
      "keep": ""
    },
    {
      "source": "E2",
      "narration": "Since the net force and mass are given, we apply Newton's second law directly to calculate the acceleration.",
      "focus": "Since F_net and m are given",
      "keep": ""
    },
    {
      "source": "E3",
      "narration": "Dividing the net force of fifty-one newtons by the mass of twenty-four kilograms gives an acceleration of two point one meters per second squared.",
      "focus": "Entering known values gives",
      "keep": ""
    },
    {
      "source": "E4",
      "narration": "The direction of the acceleration matches the net force, and vertical forces cancel since no vertical acceleration occurs.",
      "focus": "The direction of the acceleration is the same direction as that of the net force",
      "keep": ""
    }
  ],
  "mistake": "Students often confuse non-zero net force with equilibrium by setting the sum of forces to zero in all directions. The correct approach is to apply Newton's law in component form because accelerating bodies have a non-zero net force."
}

{
  "scenes": [
    {
      "source": "S2",
      "mode": "explain",
      "narration": "Mass is an intrinsic property of an object and represents the quantity of matter it contains.",
      "focus": "Mass is an intrinsic property",
      "keep": "Mass is an intrinsic property of an object that does not vary."
    },
    {
      "source": "S1",
      "mode": "explain",
      "narration": "Weight is the gravitational force acting on an object, directed downward toward the center of the Earth.",
      "focus": "commonly called its weight",
      "keep": "Weight is the downward gravitational force acting on an object of a certain mass."
    },
    {
      "source": "S4",
      "mode": "explain",
      "narration": "Weight is equal to mass multiplied by the acceleration of gravity, and its units are Newtons.",
      "focus": "",
      "keep": "Weight is directly proportional to mass when the acceleration of gravity is constant."
    },
    {
      "source": "S5",
      "mode": "explain",
      "narration": "When an object rests on an incline, the force of gravity is divided into perpendicular and parallel components.",
      "focus": "",
      "keep": ""
    }
  ],
  "example": [
    {
      "source": "E1",
      "narration": "We project all forces onto a coordinate system where one axis is parallel to the slope and the other is perpendicular to it.",
      "focus": "project all forces onto a coordinate system",
      "keep": ""
    },
    {
      "source": "E2",
      "narration": "Choose axes parallel and perpendicular to the slope to separate the problem into two simpler one dimensional parts.",
      "focus": "coordinate parallel to the slope",
      "keep": ""
    },
    {
      "source": "E3",
      "narration": "The parallel component of weight is found using the sine of the angle, and the perpendicular component uses the cosine.",
      "focus": "",
      "keep": ""
    }
  ],
  "mistake": "Students often confuse mass with weight by thinking mass depends on distance from the center of the Earth. In reality, mass is an intrinsic property that remains constant, while weight is the gravitational force that changes depending on your location."
}

{
  "scenes": [
    {
      "source": "S1",
      "mode": "explain",
      "narration": "Whenever one body exerts a force on a second body, the first body experiences a force equal in magnitude and opposite in direction.",
      "focus": "Whenever one body exerts a force on a second body, the first body experiences a force",
      "keep": "Forces always occur in pairs in equal and opposite directions."
    },
    {
      "source": "S4",
      "mode": "explain",
      "narration": "For every force acting as an action, there is an equal and opposite reaction force acting on a different object.",
      "focus": "",
      "keep": "Action and reaction forces act on different objects."
    },
    {
      "source": "S2",
      "mode": "explain",
      "narration": "The action and reaction forces are always equal in magnitude but opposite in direction, acting on different bodies so they do not cancel.",
      "focus": "always equal in magnitude but opposite in direction",
      "keep": ""
    },
    {
      "source": "S7",
      "mode": "listen",
      "narration": "",
      "focus": "Newton's third law states that forces always come in equal and opposite pairs. One way we can write that is if you imagine two objects, object 1 and object 2, the force exerted by object 1 on object 2 is equal and opposite to the force exerted by object 2 on object 1.",
      "keep": "Real forces always arise from a physical interaction between objects."
    }
  ],
  "example": [
    {
      "source": "E1",
      "narration": "We define the system as the professor, cart, and equipment. The professor pushes backward with one hundred fifty newtons, so the floor exerts an equal forward reaction force on the system.",
      "focus": "According to Newton’s third law, the floor exerts a forward reaction force of $150\\text{ N}$ on System 1.",
      "keep": ""
    },
    {
      "source": "E2",
      "narration": "The net external force is one hundred fifty newtons minus twenty four newtons of friction, giving one hundred twenty six newtons. Dividing by the total mass of eighty four kilograms yields an acceleration of one point five meters per second squared.",
      "focus": "",
      "keep": ""
    },
    {
      "source": "E3",
      "narration": "Forces between internal components like the hands and the cart do not contribute to the net external force because they are internal to the system.",
      "focus": "",
      "keep": ""
    }
  ],
  "mistake": "A common mistake is believing reaction forces reinforce the action in the same direction and are greater in magnitude. The correct answer is that every experienced reaction force is equal in magnitude and opposite in direction to the exerted action force."
}

{
  "scenes": [
    {
      "source": "S1",
      "mode": "explain",
      "narration": "In a two body pursuit problem, the motions of the objects are coupled, meaning the unknown we seek depends on the motion of both objects.",
      "focus": "the motions of the objects are coupled",
      "keep": ""
    },
    {
      "source": "S2",
      "mode": "explain",
      "narration": "A two body pursuit problem is a kinematics problem in which the unknowns are calculated by solving the kinematic equations simultaneously for two moving objects.",
      "focus": "calculating unknowns by solving the kinematic equations simultaneously",
      "keep": ""
    },
    {
      "source": "S3",
      "mode": "explain",
      "narration": "A classic problem is the Atwood machine, consisting of a rope running over a pulley with two objects of different mass attached.",
      "focus": "consists of a rope running over a pulley with two objects",
      "keep": "An Atwood machine consists of a rope running over a pulley with two objects attached."
    },
    {
      "source": "S6",
      "mode": "explain",
      "narration": "This diagram shows two blocks coupled by a rope over a pulley, with one block on a horizontal table and one hanging vertically.",
      "focus": "",
      "keep": "Two blocks can be coupled by a rope over a pulley with one on a table."
    }
  ],
  "example": [
    {
      "source": "E1",
      "narration": "Find the acceleration of the blocks and the tension in the string in terms of mass one, mass two, and acceleration due to gravity.",
      "focus": "Find the acceleration of the blocks and the tension in the string",
      "keep": ""
    },
    {
      "source": "E2",
      "narration": "We draw a free body diagram for each mass separately and apply Newton's second law to write two vector equations, noting that tension is the same throughout the string.",
      "focus": "draw a free-body diagram for each mass separately",
      "keep": ""
    },
    {
